    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **275 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**, including the new `Cp14Tests`.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe city cap is now enforced on `POST /api/owners`, rejecting with **400** once the owner's city already contains 8 owners. Following the existing OfficeFloor function-injection pattern (mirroring the sibling `DailyOwnerLimit` rule):\n\n1. **`CheckCityOwnerLimit`** \u2014 a check function that counts existing owners whose city matches the new owner's city case-insensitively, and throws `CityOwnerLimitException` when the count is \u2265 8.\n2. **`CityOwnerLimitException`** \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it flows through the escalation mechanism.\n3. **`CityOwnerLimitExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds 400 Bad Request with a `ProblemDetail`, consistent with the daily-limit handler.\n4. **Escalation YAML** \u2014 `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.CityOwnerLimitException.yml` wiring the exception to its handler.\n5. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted a `checkCityLimit` step (under the `transaction` governance) into `owners.POST.yml`, between the daily-limit check and `normalizeCity`.\n\nThe check matches cities case-insensitively so the cap holds regardless of spelling, and it runs before `NormalizeCity` alongside the other guard steps.",
